---
import ProjectPageLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectPageLayout.astro';
import SpecTable from '../../components/SpecTable.astro';
import MetricCallout from '../../components/MetricCallout.astro';
import PdfViewer from '../../components/PdfViewer.astro';

const specRows = [
  { label: 'Course',    value: 'Engr. 1, Intro. to Rocketry (E1RK)'                    },
  { label: 'Mentors',   value: 'Nicholas Veracruz (SpaceX, Relativity) · Caitlyn Hill (SpaceX)' },
  { label: 'Design',    value: 'SolidWorks · OpenRocket · Theodore von Kármán'         },
  { label: 'Build',     value: 'Fiberglass Layup · FDM · Laser Cut'                    },
  { label: 'Material',  value: 'PLA · Fiberglass (body) · Birch (fins)'                },
  { label: 'Color',     value: 'Sky Blue'                                               },
];
---
<ProjectPageLayout
  title="Group High Powered Rocket"
  description="Fiberglass high-powered rocket with 3D-printed nose cone and boat tail, designed in OpenRocket and SolidWorks for UCLA's E1RK course."
  ghostText="E1RK GMPR"
  category="E1RK — UCLA"
  tagline="Fiberglass airframe, 3D-printed nose cone &amp; boat tail"
  showOverview={false}
  nextHref="/projects/obstacle-avoidance-robot"
>
  <!-- Overview + cover image — keep current image, no figcaption -->
  <section class="spec-block" style="grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.5fr; align-items: start; gap: 1.5rem;">
    <div class="spec-col">
      <h2 class="section-overview">Overview</h2>
      <SpecTable rows={specRows} title="Specifications" />
    </div>
    <div class="cover-wrap">
      <img src="/assets/projects/gmpr/gmpr_cover.jpg"
           alt="Group high-powered rocket — assembled fiberglass airframe"
           loading="lazy" class="cover-img" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="metrics-row" aria-label="Key metrics">
    <MetricCallout value="2"   unit="rockets"  description="Rockets built"        />
    <MetricCallout value="1"   unit="launches" description="Successful launches"  />
    <MetricCallout value="12"  unit="members"  description="Team members"         />
  </section>

  <section class="the-work">
    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Constraints</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          Designed to a 3,000 ft apogee with a 1.5 to 2.5 cal stability margin, sub-15.5 sec ascent, and 75+ ft/s off-the-rail velocity, while flying and recovering a raw egg intact and logging in-flight apogee, inertia, and stability data within a ~436 g loaded mass budget.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Approach</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          Using OpenRocket and SolidWorks, we chose a Von Karman nose cone for low transonic drag (~Mach 0.7), a fiberglass airframe, and three trapezoidal birch-plywood fins for passive stability. Doubled the nose cone as the payload bay and designed a fastener-free nose cone cap from PLA clips that snap into notches, with fluff for shock isolation.
        </p>
        <!-- nosecone_cap prototype + CAD — both portrait 3:4, side by side -->
        <div class="proj-gallery proj-gallery--2">
          <figure class="proj-figure">
            <img src="/assets/projects/gmpr/nosecone_cap_prototype.jpg"
                 alt="Functional prototype of the nose cone mechanical push-up cap"
                 loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio: 3/4; object-fit: cover;" />
            <figcaption>Functional prototype of the nose cone's mechanical push-up cap</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="proj-figure">
            <img src="/assets/projects/gmpr/nosecone_cap_CAD.jpg"
                 alt="CAD of the nose cone cap slit design" loading="lazy"
                 style="aspect-ratio: 3/4; object-fit: cover;" />
            <figcaption>CAD of the nose cone cap's slit</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Build</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          Laid the fiberglass airframe, laser-cut the centering rings, bulkhead, and fin slots, and FDM-printed the nose cone and clip cap in PLA. Iterated the nose cone twice (resized per the OpenRocket optimization, then reprinted after a loose fit) and the retention cap twice more after it spun in its notch and cracked.
        </p>
        <!-- body_tube_layup (4:3) + layup_d (portrait 3:4) — side by side, 4:3 crop -->
        <div class="proj-gallery proj-gallery--2">
          <figure class="proj-figure">
            <img src="/assets/projects/gmpr/body_tube_layup.jpeg"
                 alt="Fiberglass body tube wet layup in progress" loading="lazy" />
          </figure>
          <figure class="proj-figure">
            <img src="/assets/projects/gmpr/layup_d.jpg"
                 alt="Composite wet layup detail" loading="lazy"
                 style="aspect-ratio: 4/3; object-fit: cover;" />
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Analysis</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          Modeled the full flight in OpenRocket (237 m/s max velocity, ~Mach 0.7, 133 Ns motor impulse) and ran a stability trade study showing that removing the nose electronics pushed the margin from ~1.45 to 1.34 cal, below the 1.5 cal floor.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Design Review</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <!-- Design review slide deck — 6 pages, arrow-navigable -->
        <PdfViewer
          src="/assets/projects/gmpr/review_slides.pdf"
          pages={6}
          label="GMPR design review slides"
          aspectRatio="16 / 9"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Result</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          After four ignition attempts caused by a loss of motor continuity, the motor fired and the rocket launched on a slightly angled but straight trajectory. Post-flight, our search failed, leaving recovery and egg condition unconfirmed.
        </p>
        <!-- launch (portrait 0.57:1) + flight_config (4:3) — side by side, 4:3 crop -->
        <div class="proj-gallery proj-gallery--2">
          <figure class="proj-figure">
            <img src="/assets/projects/gmpr/launch.PNG"
                 alt="Rocket on the launchpad for flight" loading="lazy"
                 style="aspect-ratio: 4/3; object-fit: cover; object-position: center 17%;" />
          </figure>
          <figure class="proj-figure">
            <img src="/assets/projects/gmpr/flight_config.jpg"
                 alt="Assembled rocket in flight configuration" loading="lazy" />
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="work-row">
      <h2 class="work-label">Reflection</h2>
      <div class="work-content">
        <p class="work-text">
          With the electronics cut during the build to protect the stability, the apogee logging requirement went unmet. Being on a two-person team (versus the typical five- to six-person team) was a challenge.
        </p>
        <figure class="proj-figure">
          <img src="/assets/projects/gmpr/mandrel.jpg"
               alt="Mandrel used for the fiberglass body tube layup" loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectPageLayout>

<style>
  .spec-col { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .spec-col :global(.section-overview) {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
    font-weight: var(--fw-black);
    color: var(--text-100);
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  /* Cover image in overview — 4:3 crop to match spec column height */
  .cover-wrap {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .cover-img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    display: block;
  }
</style>
